SM(553,{"n":"1 Corinthians 15 Conclusion","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 15 Conclusion.mp3","d":2883.4,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.44,"Verse 42. So also is the resurrection of the dead."],[25.84,"The body is sown in corruption, it is raised in incorruption."],[30.24,"It is sown in dishonor, it is raised in glory, it is sown in weakness, it is raised in power."],[37.68,"So St. Paul's making a series of contrasts."],[40.1,"He's start using this sown language."],[41.94,"So he's going back to his first metaphor, which was about plants."],[45.8,"And this farming metaphor."],[47.92,"So all of these things in the first set of contrasts are true of our bodies now."],[53.76,"Our bodies now are corrupt."],[56.16,"Our bodies now manifest the effects of our sin and the sin in the world."],[59.28,"They are dishonored"],[62.36,"They are corrupt."],[63.24,"They are not pure."],[67.4,"And he contrasts dishonor with glory."],[70.84,"Meaning, because of that sinfulness and because of that corruption, we are not now, we are now outside of paradise."],[78.76,"We're cut off from the cut off from the presence of God."],[83.8,"It is sown in weakness, it is raised in power."],[87.32,"So now we are weak."],[90.28,"We have aches, we have pains, we have suffering."],[93.24,"We have, you know, as uh Christ said to his disciples when they couldn't pray with him."],[98.12,"Spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak."],[100.24,"As Saint Paul talked about in his epistle to the Romans, you know, the good we want we to want. to do, we don't do, the evil don't do, we end up doing"],[100.44,"St. We have this weakness that's part of our uh mortal and uh and corrupt bodies."],[117.76,"And so uh you see sort of the the escalation here then in the contrast."],[123.29,"That corruption is replaced with incorruption."],[125.85,"Our bodies are purified, they're now incorrupt."],[128.49,"Our dishonor is replaced with glory."],[130.83,"We're able to again draw close to God and be in the presence of God."],[134.67,"Our bodies are sown in weakness and they're raised in power."],[138.17,"That word power there is power or authority."],[141.21,"And it's not coincidental that those last two, where he talks about glory and power, those two ideas are why he introduced the concept of the angelic beings in the first place."],[157.25,"The first one being the glory."],[158.69,"He talked about how they differ from each other in glory, the ranks, what we would call the ranks of angels, uh, in our present uh sort of orthodox usual terminology."],[169.17,"They differ from each other in glory and how close they are they are to God."],[173.65,"You will notice uh in the icons of the saints, what we got have gotten used to in the West is calling a halo, that's actually a nimbus around uh around the saint's face."],[186.59,"This is uh supposed to be the idea reflect the idea that they in the presence of God are now reflecting the glory of God."],[194.79,"Uh it is not their own glory, but it is God's glory reflected in them, in which they're participating because they're in his presence."],[203.43,"And sort of exhibit A of that, of course, is back in the Torah when when Moses goes and speaks to God face to face on Mount Sinai, when he comes back, his face is shining with the glory of God."],[216.31,"And uh, and so he has to the the the Israelites can't stand the glory of God, it's painful to them uh because of their present sinful state of being."],[227.03,"And so, and so Moses has to could wear a veil and hide it and hide it from them."],[231.99,"But this is true of the saints who be who are there uh with chistr face to face all the time and we reflect that in our iconography this is why that the nimbus around christ's head is different and reflects both his identity it has the name of the Lord there in Greek transliteration Yahweh identifying him and it has the form of the cross uh because of course if you read Saint John's gospel that's where Christ is glorified is in his death as in his crucifixion that's the terminology that Saint John uses for it is his glorification."],[266.83,"So that's Christ's own glory."],[268.79,"It's not reflected."],[270.47,"But the saints, it is it is reflected."],[273.99,"And so for Saint Paul, in the same way that those angelic beings have different bodies."],[281.27,"They appear differently."],[282.87,"When Isaiah or Ezekiel see them, they look different than each other."],[287.75,"That different participation requires a different a different bodily form."],[292.63,"In the same way, this destiny that we have in Christ to be in the presence of God, to be transformed by the presence of God, to have him glorify us by allowing us to participate in his glory, which is is reflected from us, that requires a transformation of our body."],[310.87,"That requires a new a new type of body for that destiny."],[316.91,"And then the third element that he mentions, power or authority, is again directly connected to this idea that not only our our bodies are going to be in the resurrection are going to be more like celestial bodies, more like angelic bodies, but our role, not merely the uh the participation's connection to the presence of God, but the role that we play in creation, authority or power, is also going to be more like the current role uh or order of these celestial beings of the angelic beings."],[357.71,"Meaning that we, like the angelic beings now, in addition to being in the presence of God, will also be uh, he will also share with us his his governance uh of the creation."],[372.73,"And that second part, of course, is where the entire uh theological uh teaching surrounding the saints comes in the Orthodox Church."],[384.01,"That this is human destiny, that the angelic beings who fell and uh became uh what we now call demonic beings uh replaced by glorified humanity uh in the in this role of governing the creation."],[402.81,"So we see this in the book of Revelation in several places, some of them subtle, some of them uh more obvious."],[409.53,"Of course, we see it in in chapter 20, where the saints in glory rule and reign with Christ."],[415.82,"Christ, we see it in Christ's promise to the twelve uh disciples, that they'll sit on twelve thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel."],[423.88,"Ruling over the twelve tribes of Israel."],[425.96,"So this language is used in terms of promise all through the script."],[429.48,"It goes back to the promises made to Abraham that his uh his descendants would be like the stars, like the angelic beings."],[437.48,"Uh it and as I mentioned, it's there in subtle ways too."],[441.08,"So in in Israel, there were 72 elders who governed uh Israel, and it's not a coincidence that when uh Saint John has his vision of heaven, uh the book of Revelation, and he sees the worship there, he sees twenty-four human elders."],[458.44,"Well, twenty-four human elders, twenty-four out of seventy-t iswo one.-third"],[462.88,"And so then when you get to Revelation 12 and you see that one-third of the angelic beings have fallen with Satan, you get the point that Saint John is making."],[473.92,"That one-third has been replaced by the saints in glory, who are now uh governing and administering the creation."],[483.36,"So Saint Paul is here setting out that role that humans are going to play as the argument that, well, the body we will have in the resurrection, the body which we will be raised with is going to be a body that's suited to those purposes."],[500.64,"In the same way that angelic beings have bodies suited to their purposes, same way animals have bodies suited to their purposes, the same way plants have bodies suited suited to the purposes for which God created them."],[515.4,"That our bodies will be suited in the same way."],[519.32,"So he goes on in verse 44."],[521.32,"It is sown a natural body, it is raised a spiritual body."],[525.32,"There is a natural body and there is a spiritual body."],[530.84,"This is uh one of the more easily confused portions of uh St."],[537.72,"Paul's discussion. The next few verses are actually."],[540.84,"But this is one that a lot of people uh have trouble with, and part of it is the way in which the English word spiritual has sort of been devalued uh in our modern world."],[552.4,"So we hear the word spiritual, and we may think of people who say that they're not religious, but they're spiritual."],[560.88,"The word spiritual to us just sort of refers to some kind of ephemeral kind of bodiless something, maybe made up of feelings or sensations or emotions or something similar."],[574.32,"And so the idea of a natural body makes sense to us."],[578.98,"Natural, okay. We've all got bodies that are natural that have natures."],[583.6,"But then what's a spiritual body?"],[585.52,"In fact, from uh a lot of pagan perspectives at this point in time, spiritual body would essentially be an oxymoron, it would be self-contradictory."],[594.64,"Body and spirit were opposites."],[595.92,"Spirit means not body by sort of by definition"],[602.2,"Uh, and therefore, body physicality would mean not spirit."],[608.68,"Uh, but as I've commented before uh in this Bible study a bunch of times, almost not every single, but almost every time you see the word spirit in the scriptures, it should have a capital S in front of it."],[623.16,"It is very rare, not completely absent, but very rare that the term spirit or spiritual is used in the scriptures just to refer to that kind of ephemeral thing or bodiless."],[637.96,"It is in a few places."],[639.24,"It is in a few places, like when uh Christ says, touch me and see, for a spirit has not flesh and bones as you see that I have."],[645.76,"That's that kind of use."],[646.64,"So it there are places, but most of the time, spirit should have a capital S in front of it."],[652.4,"So what St."],[654.08,"Paul is contrasting here is a body which is characterized by nature, by a certain nature, and a body which is characterized by the spirit, meaning the Holy Spirit, meaning the spirit of God."],[671.6,"These are the two kinds of bodies he's he's talking about."],[675.6,"And this is sort of a fine-tuning of what he said before when he talked about terrestrial bodies and celestial bodies, terrestrial bodies being created thingsings on earth, celestial bodies being angelic be."],[685.92,"He's building off of that a little bit"],[692.52,"So our natural body."],[694.54,"Our body that we are now is part of the nature of this world, of the uh visible world in which we spend most of our time."],[707.5,"It is subject to all the processes, that that everything else in the natural world is subject to."],[714.99,"My circulatory system is different to my dog's circulatory system, but it's not that much different."],[720.99,"We're both mammals."],[722.27,"And so it is part of our bodies now, are part of this age, part of this creation, part of this world."],[732.63,"St. Paul is saying that the body with which we're raised is going to be characterized not primarily by its connection to this world and this creation, but it's going to be characterized primarily by the way it is permeated by the Holy Spirit."],[751.83,"That the Holy Spirit who now dwells within us, God Himself, who dwells within us as Christians, as people who have been chrismated, as people who've been anointed, have been baptized with the Spirit, is now not only going to dwell within us as a temple, but it's going to permeate us."],[773.75,"What exactly does that mean?"],[775.55,"I don't know."],[776.51,"Neither did St."],[777.63,"Paul. So this isn't some kind of spiritual science with that that crosses all the T's and dots all the I's."],[785.95,"But what St."],[787.15,"Paul is saying here about our bodies is the same thing that St."],[792.67,"John is saying in Revelation chapter 21 about the creation."],[797.55,"Because what does he say about the creation?"],[800.67,"He says that in the new creation, there's no temple."],[804.59,"There's no temple because God Hsimelf will dwell with us forever."],[808.67,"St. Saint Paul has just used the language earlier in chapter 15 of God the God being all in all after Christ's glorious appearing."],[818.67,"In uh the Old Testament. prophets, it talks about how the knowledge of God will cover the earth as the water covers the sea"],[827.03,"So, in the same way that on that cosmic level, the prophets and Saint John talk about God's presence in his creation in the age to come, in the world to come, being filling all things, in the same way, the our body is going to go from being the temple of the Holy Spirit, as Saint Paul said earlier in 1 Corinthians, to being permeated and characterized by the Holy Spirit, being transformed by the Spirit."],[855.35,"And so that's that's the analogy that Saint Paul is drawing."],[858.63,"He can't beat in on it."],[860.39,"He's going to say, we can't fully understand what all this means now."],[864.79,"But we have these images and metaphors that can help us understand it"],[871.63,"But but the whole reality of it is beyond our comprehension now."],[876.19,"And ultimately we get to a point where we have to just stop and give glory and thanks and praise to God for what we for the great things he has in store for us, which we can't now fully understand."],[889.23,"Verse 45, and so it is written the first man Adam became a living being, the last Adam became a life-giving spirit."],[899.07,"So this verse, 1 Corinthians 15, 45, is infamously difficult."],[905.31,"I mentioned, I think, uh last time that uh in one of my uh Protestant seminary classes, the whole assignment for the term was to write a fairly lengthy paper on this verse, on how it should be translated and what it means."],[922.63,"Because it's infamously difficult."],[924.07,"I just read it from the Orthodox uh study Bible, which is basically in this case, it's the New King James."],[930.63,"I'm about to in our discussion sort of retranslate it, because as it's translated, it's really, really confusing."],[938.55,"Especially the part about the last the last Adam becoming a life-giving spirit."],[943.75,"And if you have your Orthodox Study Bible and are looking at it, you may notice that in in that second clause, the word became as in italics."],[952.63,"That's because it's not there in the original."],[954.55,"It's assumed Now you may also notice if you're looking at it in your Orthodox Study Bible that the phrase is in quotation marks and in italics, the first man Adam became a life, became a living being."],[969.79,"And that is because it is a translation from Genesis 2, verse 7."],[976.51,"It's a quotation from the for that's very clearly from the Greek, just for anybody keeping track of when St."],[983.87,"Paul quotes the Hebrew and does his own translation, which he does fairly often, or when he just quotes uh the existing uh Greek translations, which uh he also does very often."],[997.47,"So the the quotation uh that St"],[997.59,"Uh in this case he's he's uh quoting the Greek."],[1004.55,"Paul makes uh when he says uh so is written the first man Adam became a living being."],[1010.63,"We've talked I've talked before a lot in the in this Bible study that we have to always remember that until the 16th century these chapter and verse breaks did not exist."],[1020.63,"That means Saint Paul could not say to the people to whom he was speaking, check out Genesis 2, verse 7."],[1027.45,"Or uh read Genesis 2, 1 through 10, in order to to make his point."],[1032.41,"If you ever look at my blog, I will f frequently throw in parentheses and then a whole list of Bible verses that you can go look up if you if you don't trust me or you just really want to dig into it."],[1043.77,"But say Paul couldn't do that."],[1045.49,"There are no verse references."],[1047.65,"So the way in which uh texts were quoted in the ancient world, the way they're quoted in the New Testament, is that you would quote uh either the first line of a section to which you were referring, and when you quote that first line that would mean someone who wanted to look it up could go to the scroll, go and find the line you referred to and then read."],[1068.93,"Or you would refer to sort of a key sentence or key verse or key part of it that would cause would ring a bell and they would say, Oh, yeah, that that story."],[1080.25,"And they'd go back and again, either way, read the whole thing."],[1084.21,"So, whenever you see uh anywhere in the New Testament, sort of a verse quoted, it's important that you go and if you want to understand what the New Testament author is doing, that you go and look at the passage from which that verse came."],[1100.41,"Because they're not just referring to that sentence, they're not just sort of proof texting or taking things out of context, they're referring back to that context."],[1109.05,"They expect you to either be familiar with it or to go and and look at it so that you know, oh yeah, that's what it's talking about."],[1116.17,"So in this case, this is from the creation of Adam."],[1119.13,"Okay, so St."],[1120.33,"Paul is taking us back to the original creation, and God has formed Adam, He's formed his body out of the dust."],[1131.63,"Out of the dust of the earth, we're told."],[1133.15,"He's formed out of clay."],[1133.79,"He's molded And then God breathes the breath of life, we're told, into his nostrils."],[1142.45,"And so the portion that St."],[1144.69,"Paul quotes, and so because of that, because of that breathing of the breath of life into him, he becomes a living being."],[1152.85,"So St. Paul starts by referring us back to how we came to have the bodies we have now, how we came to be the beings who we are now."],[1163.41,"How we came to have our life in this world in this age."],[1168.45,"It was the gift of God at this event."],[1170.69,"Okay So this is our picture of how the natural body came to be."],[1175.17,"This is our picture, uh, not yet, you're gonna have to go to Genesis. 3 to see how that body then became corrupt, but and those and weak and those other things"],[1184.65,"But this is how this body uh came to be."],[1188.57,"Now, the second half of verse 45, then, is gonna be Saint Paul telling us how the new body he's talking about, how the resurrection body that he's talking about came into being."],[1205.29,"And so it is regarding that that he says the last Adam, and again, New King James inserts, became a life-giving spirit."],[1216.25,"So that word became, isn't there?"],[1217.93,"It just says the last Adam, and then what they're translating as a lifeiving- spgirit."],[1223.53,"998% of the time, spirit needs a capital S."],[1224.45,"Remember what I said about the previous verse."],[1234.37,"So what it literally says there is the last Adam, verb assumed, not there."],[1241.49,"The last Adam, the spirit who gives life with a capital S."],[1248.05,"So, who is the last Adam?"],[1250.29,"Well, in St."],[1251.25,"Paul, he's already talked about this in 1 Corinthians, made this connection between Adam and Christ."],[1260.21,"Death entering through Adam, life through Christ."],[1262.61,"He's done it earlier in this very chapter."],[1264.61,"He also does this in in uh Romans 5."],[1267.57,"St. Paul makes the same comparison between Adam and Christ with death and life and eternal life."],[1274.89,"So the last Adam, we're talking about Christ here."],[1277.93,"So Adam is the first man from whom all of us have inherited our nature, our na including our natural body, in this world and in this life."],[1290.41,"We are what we are through descent from Adam."],[1294.25,"That's why we're mortal."],[1296.25,"That's why we're corruptible."],[1298.57,"That's why we're weak."],[1300.09,"All of these things."],[1301.29,"So now Christ is being set up"],[1301.57,"He's the progenitor."],[1306.25,"Christ is being set up as the progenitor, the body, the nature that we will have in the resurrection."],[1313.29,"So St Paul is also building off of what he said earlier in 1 Corinthians 15, which is about the intimate connection between Christ's resurrection from the dead and our eventual resurrection from the dead."],[1327.09,"So Saint Paul is here saying that the body he's been talking about, the spiritual body, the body characterized by the Spirit, begins the first, the sort of the last Adam or the first man of the new creation is Christ's humanity."],[1344.21,"Christ's humanity in the resurrection."],[1347.33,"So our uh body in the resurrection is related to Christ's body and his resurrection in the same way that our body now is related to Adam's body."],[1358.45,"Paul is setting up"],[1360.73,"That's the parallel that St."],[1364.09,"And so we don't need to insert uh the word became here."],[1368.81,"The verb that's translated became in the first half of the verse."],[1374.49,"First Adam became, first man became a living being, can mean, it's it's uh Yenome in Greek, can mean become."],[1385.93,"It could also mean come, arrive, enter into, appear, start to exist."],[1393.45,"There's a number of ways it can be translated."],[1396.17,"But with the parallel that St."],[1398.01,"Paul is making, if you go to Genesis 2, what happens?"],[1401.21,"The breath of life from God enters, it comes into Adam's nostrils, and that makes him now a living being."],[1410.29,"That's what transforms him, that's what creates, that's what makes the natural body."],[1416.05,"The spiritual body, which is the body which is permeated by the Spirit, that is characterized by the Holy Spirit, that St."],[1422.29,"Paul is saying we will have in the resurrection, begins with Christ when what happens?"],[1427.09,"When the Spirit who gives life, when the Holy Spirit enter theent places says that all three persons of Trinity were the agent of Christ's resurrection."],[1442.57,"He says that Christ rose."],[1444.27,"St. John says the same thing."],[1445.71,"St. John, in uh St."],[1447.87,"John's Gospel, Christ says, No one takes my life from me, I lay it down, I can take it up again."],[1454.11,"St. Paul says that the Father raised Christ from the dead."],[1457.95,"He says, not just here, that the Holy Spirit raised Christ from the dead."],[1462.19,"That this is an action of the entire Holy Trinity, the resurrection of Christ."],[1466.91,"And so here he's focusing on the Spirit who gives life."],[1471.0,"The Holy Spirit as the agent of Christ's resurrection, and therefore involved in our own resurrection."],[1477.48,"This is why the whole key here is the fact that we as Christians, we as people who have been baptized, have been baptized into Christ, put on Christ in St."],[1490.92,"Paul's terminology, and and that we have then part parcel that is receiving the Holy Spirit within us."],[1498.24,"So there is a sense in which we talked last time about how for Saint Paul, all of these promises are already true of us."],[1506.8,"They've already been received by us."],[1509.12,"In his epistle to the Ephesians, he's going to say we're already seated with Christ in the heavenly places."],[1514.08,"We're already ruling and reigning with him while we're alive in this world."],[1519.6,"All these promises are already ours in a sense, but at the same time, again, we talked about potency and reality, at the same time, they are not yet fully realized from our perspective."],[1534.48,"We are already resurrected in a certain sense"],[1534.76,"And so for St."],[1535.8,"Paul, the same thing is true of the resurrection."],[1541.96,"We are already resurrected in the sense that we have already received the Holy Spirit."],[1547.32,"We have already died and risen with Christ in baptism."],[1549.96,"We've already put on Christ."],[1551.56,"And we're awaiting the reality of that to be reflected in our souls and bodies at Christ's glorious appearing."],[1559.48,"So all that is to say, I would and have, at one point on my blog, I would translate this verse more like, and so it is written: the first man Adam became a living being, and into the last Adam came a life-giving spirit."],[1575.56,"Would be how I would translate that to bring out uh into into the last Adam came the Spirit who gives life."],[1582.04,"I'm sorry, uh, to sort of make the point that that St."],[1582.44,"So he goes on in verse 46"],[1585.2,"Paul is making in English."],[1588.96,"However, the spiritual is not first, but the natural, and afterward the spiritual."],[1593.6,"So he says there's an order to things."],[1596.1,"There's an order to things."],[1597.94,"And this verse is also important."],[1600.26,"The point he's making here about the order of things, about this progression, is important when we're talking to certain people that are our Mormon friends from the Church of Jesus Christ, the Latter-day Saints, being probably the most common modern people, but there were originists in the ancient world and Gnostics and others, even plate platonists of a lot of varieties, who uh had the view of the the pre-existence of souls, that we had some previous divine or semi-divine existence, and then we fell into this current natural state, and then we're going to escape it or uh supersede it in the future."],[1636.22,"St. Paul is wiping that whole idea out."],[1640.24,"The natural comes first."],[1642.1,"When we were born into this world, that's when we came into existence, period."],[1646.2,"And now the spiritual is what comes next."],[1649.88,"Is what comes next if we're in Christ."],[1652.44,"The first man was of the earth, made of dust, the second man is the Lord from heaven."],[1658.12,"So he's he's drawing out what he just said in verses in verse 45."],[1663.25,"So that that first man, he's made out of the earth, he's made out of this creation."],[1667.81,"If you read closely in Genesis 2. God creates him out of the earth and then takes him and puts him in paradise"],[1675.85,"Man wasn't created in paradise."],[1677.69,"He wasn't created in the presence of God."],[1679.45,"He was created in this world, as part of the creation of this world, and then he's placed in paradise."],[1685.61,"And then after his transgression is removed from paradise back into this present world."],[1693.29,"And the second man, and this is important again for anybody who wants to try and say that uh Saint Paul didn't believe uh that uh Christ is God, but he says, the second man is the Lord from heaven."],[1708.33,"The reason why the Lord from heaven is important is that uh in most cases. in the Greek, and the Greek translation of the Old Testament being what St"],[1720.45,"Paul just quoted, and if you go and look at uh Genesis 2 and compare the Hebrew and the Greek, you'll see that in the Hebrew uh the name Yahweh is used uh for God in Genesis 2, and uh in the Greek translation, uh that name uh rather than being transliterated as it is in some places as Oon or something else, is uh translated as Kyrios."],[1747.41,"It's replaced with Lord."],[1749.25,"So when he says Christ is the Lord from heaven, the from heaven is making it very clear."],[1756.13,"He's not just saying Christ is the Lord in the sense of Lord or Master or ruler."],[1760.41,"When he says the Lord from heaven, he's making it very clear that he's saying Christ is Yahweh who came from his heavenly place to become man."],[1772.97,"It is abundantly clear that that is what St."],[1775.61,"Paul is referring to."],[1776.41,"And he's writing this in the mid-50s."],[1778.49,"So anyone who wants to tell you that the idea that Jesus was God is something that developed over the course of a couple of centuries is all wet."],[1788.81,"St. Paul uh presupposes everywhere."],[1791.29,"We've already seen uh back in chapter 8, uh, we've already seen how uh Saint Paul inserted the person of Jesus Christ into the Shema, into the prayer of Israel, Hero Israel, the Lord your God, Yahweh your God, Yahweh is one."],[1806.69,"He's inserted Christ into that."],[1808.53,"So it's very clear here in 1 Corinthians that Saint Paul sees Christ as Yahweh, the God of Israel incarnate."],[1815.97,"So he goes on, as was the man of dust, so also are those who are made of dust, and as is the heavenly man, so also are those who are heavenly."],[1826.37,"As we have borne the image of the man of dust, we shall also bear the image of the heavenly man."],[1832.85,"So just as we've borne the image of Adam, uh, in fact, at the end of uh Genesis three, we're told that uh Adam uh brought forth a son in his own image."],[1846.39,"So as we've borne the image of Adam, as we've had a a body like his, soul like his, as we've lived our lives in the world as he did, so also Saint Paul is telling us we will be like Christ in the resurrection."],[1862.43,"And again, he's specifically speaking here about how our body will be."],[1868.27,"Not just we'll be like him in terms of moral virtue or knowledge or whatever, but that we will we will be our bodies will be like Christ's body uh in the resurrection."],[1882.35,"Now, this I say, brethren, that flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God, nor does corruption inherit incorruption."],[1889.39,"So I mentioned this before with Saint Paul."],[1891.95,"St. Saint Paul has a number of important distinctions. he makes that are sometimes lost in English, at least to us as readers of modern English"],[1901.35,"One of those that's very pertinent here is the difference between how Saint Paul uses the word body and the way Saint Paul uses the word flesh."],[1910.07,"So you may notice in this last verse he has been talking about bodies."],[1914.39,"So we're talking about Christ's body, our body, the b angelic bodies, animal bodies, plant bodies."],[1919.27,"So we're talking about all the different types of bodies and now all of a sudden he says flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God."],[1926.39,"So he's not here talking about no body, nothing bodily can enter the kingdom of God, as if he's saying, you know, well, we have to die, and then our soul goes off and or our spirit goes off and enters the kingdom of God once we escape this body."],[1939.91,"He's talking about the flesh"],[1940.51,"He's not now contradicting himself."],[1943.71,"When Saint Paul talks about the flesh, he's talking about the sinful and corrupt element of our body."],[1949.95,"And blood, in the old test, throughout the Old Testament, blood is life."],[1953.95,"We're told over and over again that the life is in the blood."],[1957.23,"And so when he talks about flesh and blood, he's talking about our life in this world and our body as it's as it's corrupt in this world."],[1965.15,"And he parallels that to make that point clear by saying that corruption does not inherit incorruption."],[1972.03,"So when he talks about flesh and blood here, he's not talking about our bodies, but the corruption of our bodies."],[1977.73,"Being unable, being unable to inherit."],[1980.69,"Because for Saint Paul, and in St."],[1982.61,"Paul's understanding, part of what happens when man is expelled from paradise is that Adam and Eve receive a different type of body."],[1991.05,"It is not just that their bodies were just like ours now, but now all of a sudden they could die."],[1997.93,"Or that uh they were always mortal."],[2000.65,"God was just making sure they didn't fall off a cliff or get trampled by an elephant or something."],[2005.13,"But they were mortal just like us."],[2007.29,"They were created, they were created immortal, they became mortal."],[2013.45,"And fathers uh focus in, and most of our Second Temple Jewish sources focus in on the episode where uh we're told that uh God makes garments of skin for Adam and Eve when they're expelled from paradise."],[2029.65,"A lot of times we read this in a very materialist way that, like, oh, well, they're naked, remember, uh, and fig leaves don't work so well, so God's showing them how to make leather clothes."],[2041.71,"But that's not how that's not how ancient sources, uh, Jewish or Christian, understood that that little part of the story."],[2049.87,"They understood that as twofold."],[2051.63,"First, God killed animals to do that, to show them what death was, to show them what they were now going to be subject to."],[2058.83,"And those garments of flesh is the same flesh, and they would have been bloody because they're skinned animals."],[2064.69,"So the flesh and blood that uh St."],[2068.05,"Paul refers to here is connected to this idea."],[2071.17,"The idea that they're given mortal bodies that are subject to corruption, subject to death, subject to pain and suffering."],[2078.02,"That that's what that's what humanity received."],[2082.34,"And the reason for that, again, as we've already mentioned tonight, is that uh God was giving mortality to man to enable repentance, to enable change, to enable transformation."],[2094.74,"Uh the demons, at the various times when they rebelled, at the various times when they decided to turn against God, there is for them no repentance."],[2103.38,"They are immortally fixed in their rebellion."],[2107.94,"But God granted this extra grace to humanity."],[2112.62,"That when uh humanity chose to rebel, when Adam and Eve chose to rebel, God did not leave them immortal."],[2117.62,"Then we would have been like the demons"],[2120.62,"He said it would not be good for them to be immortal."],[2124.86,"We would have just been permanently and forever in rebellion against God."],[2130.3,"But he gave us, he gave us our life in this world, he gave us mortality, which brings with it mutability and changeability, the ability to suffer and be transformed and to change and to repent."],[2142.46,"He gave that to us as a gift."],[2144.7,"This is what Saint John of Damascus is talking about in his hymns in our funeral service, when he talks about how he turned us back to the ground from which we were taken to entreat repose, that this is why we're given over to death."],[2159.76,"So that we could have this repentance. so that the destiny God created us for"],[2165.0,"Saint Paul's just been talking about this great destiny that God created us for, that we will receive in full in the resurrection."],[2172.2,"That's what we were created for in the first place."],[2174.52,"So that we would not might not lose that."],[2177.4,"God gave us mortality so that we could repent."],[2180.76,"Now, unfortunately, we're present what's presented in Scripture is that not everyone will take this opportunity to repent."],[2187.96,"That there are some who will remain hardened in rebellion against God."],[2192.44,"And so at the time of the end, when they face eternal condemnation, they will go on to share the fate, the fate of uh the demonic beings, because they continue to share in their rebellion."],[2205.48,"They continue in their hatred of God."],[2208.08,"But for those who repent, God has appointed forgiveness and has kept that destiny for which he created us open."],[2215.92,"And that's what St."],[2216.96,"Paul is talking about here."],[2218.64,"He's talking about the fact that it's not just a question of for us to be transformed, this needs to die."],[2227.2,"It's that this needs to, this needs to die because the flesh and blood, because the mortality, because the corruption need to be gotten rid of, need to be taken away now."],[2240.32,"Uh, death will have served its purpose."],[2243.76,"Death doesn't disappear, but death now has no sting."],[2247.76,"Death now has no victory, uh, St."],[2250.48,"Paul is going to say."],[2251.76,"No longer has. power over us"],[2253.64,"Rather, it serves its original purpose of purifying us and delivering us from our sin and corruption, and delivering us into the salvation of God."],[2264.92,"Behold, I tell you a mystery."],[2266.84,"We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed."],[2269.72,"In a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet, for the trumpet will sound, and the dead will be raised incorruptible, and we shall be changed."],[2279.48,"For this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal must put on immortality."],[2285.16,"So Saint Paul is saying we won't all sleep, meaning we won't, not everyone will die on this earth."],[2291.0,"There will be a last generation at Christ's glorious appearing, who was alive at the time."],[2297.12,"He's returning now to this theme of the general resurrection and the last judgment of when Christ appears, when Christ is present with us again."],[2305.36,"When that happens, there will be people who haven't yet physically died, but they too will be changed in the same way."],[2318.64,"And then Saint Paul talks about this last trumpet."],[2322.56,"I'll go into this, I'll go into this a little bit."],[2324.72,"This is a theme with uh St."],[2326.16,"Paul. St. Paul also talks about this uh in uh 2 Thessalonians."],[2331.2,"Uh, he refers to this trumpet that sounds uh at Christ's return."],[2336.32,"And the reason he's talking about a trumpet uh and that this is associated, is that the feast of trumpets, uh, what's called the feast of trumpets in the Old Testament is actually what is now called the the Jewish holiday of uh Rosh Hashanah, which is literally means the head or the first of the year."],[2356.52,"Uh it's the New Year, the New Year's celebration."],[2359.96,"Uh, it is uh date wise on the Old Testament calendar, it's the first day of the month of Tishri."],[2368.12,"And the first day of the month of Tishri is important for a whole pile of reasons."],[2373.0,"It's the the day Noah was born, it's the day the flood ended, and uh the the world began again after the flood, it's the day that David was uh crowned as king of Judah."],[2387.0,"h And the day that all the kings of Judah after that, which would of course include the Messia, would include Christ, were crowned and began their reign, was on first Tishri."],[2398.16,"And so this idea came to surround the idea of the day of the Lord."],[2402.4,"When the age to come begins, it will begin with this trumpet blast to signal that this new age is beginning."],[2408.72,"That the age of the Messiah is beginning."],[2410.96,"Christ's full reign over the entirety of creation is beginning."],[2415.68,"And it core that date corresponds to somewhere in the depending on the, of course, the Jewish calendar is a lunar calendar, so it bounces around a little bit."],[2423.92,"But somewhere in the early half of September."],[2427.6,"And it's related to this, why we have our church new year in the Orthodox Church on September 1st."],[2433.72,"That it's it's a parallel, it's a parallel uh point."],[2437.72,"There are some people who argue that who want to argue based on largely all those things I just said, plus a really interesting interpretation of Revelation chapter 12, that Christ was actually born on on first Tishri in uh 3 BC."],[2453.32,"But when we get to Revelation chapter 12 in the Bible study, I will talk about that."],[2457.32,"I'm not totally convinced, but I'll at least tell you about it."],[2460.6,"Um what that theory is."],[2462.6,"But so that's that's the idea here."],[2465.0,"That's why St."],[2465.72,"Paul associates this trumpet blast with that means it's the beginning of the new age."],[2472.04,"Christ is now uh has now been enthroned as king, is going to judge the world. he's going to judge the living and the dead"],[2479.44,"Uh it's sort of the signal, and so he uses it as sort of a symbol of the whole end of this age and the beginning of the age to come and the resurrection."],[2489.44,"And so, in the same way that Adam and Eve were clothed in in garments of flesh, were clothed in mortality, now our mortality is going to be clothed with immortality."],[2502.82,"This is going to be reversed."],[2504.74,"Uh and so Saint Paul is associating first history with the new creation."],[2508.66,"Oh That oh that's another thing with which first history uh it was uh thought to be the sixth day of creation on which humanity was created."],[2518.62,"Especially pertinent here since St."],[2520.26,"Paul's just been talking about the creation of Adam."],[2522.02,"So now he's associating the general resurrection with that same day"],[2528.46,"Same kind of idea of a new sixth day of creation, when we are recreated in the resurrection."],[2535.66,"Verse 54, so when this corruptible has put on incorruption, and this mortal has put on immortality, then shall be brought to pass the saying that is written Death is swallowed up in victory."],[2547.9,"O death, where is your sting?"],[2549.26,"O Hades, where is your victory?"],[2551.18,"The sting of death is sin, and the strength of sin is the law."],[2554.86,"But thanks be to God who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ."],[2559.02,"So he says, This is this is when this is going to be brought to completion, this sort of already and not yet idea."],[2566.0,"So when it happens, when the resurrection happens, and when our mortal flesh is put on incorruption."],[2572.34,"Sin is gone, death is gone."],[2574.66,"Uh the powers, the uh powers of evil, spiritual powers of evil who are rayed against us, have been put down once and for all."],[2582.42,"And Christ is all in all."],[2584.9,"He hands over the kingdom to his Father."],[2587.3,"We have life eternal in the world to come."],[2589.86,"This is when this will truly be fulfilled."],[2592.75,"That death has no victory."],[2594.59,"Death has no sting."],[2596.27,"Uh Hades, the grave, um, has no victory."],[2601.01,"Because Christ has conquered them all and dispatched them all, and they're all gone."],[2606.13,"Those three enemies that we talked about last time have all been finally and permanently. defeated and destroyed"],[2612.49,"Notice he says the sting of death is sin, and the strength of sin is the law."],[2618.49,"And he's already talked to us a little bit about that in terms of the law, in terms of the Torah, you know, which uh what does he mean?"],[2626.89,"The strength of the strength of sin is the law."],[2630.49,"Uh, well, the law shows us sin."],[2633.79,"The law shows us sin."],[2635.71,"And so uh it points us to the power of sin."],[2640.11,"Uh as St."],[2640.83,"Paul said in Romans, also reading the commandments led him to temptation, because all those things he read, he wasn't supposed to do, he started to do."],[2648.03,"Uh he says that doesn't make the commandment evil, uh, that makes him evil, uh, and the commandment good."],[2654.51,"Um, and so of course, the sting, uh, the sting of death is sin"],[2654.67,"Uh, what is what is uh a sting?"],[2663.03,"Well, the sting of an animal or the bite of a snake, the sting of a scorpion, uh a spider bite or sting, uh is uh a wound that inflicts you with poison."],[2673.67,"Poison that will kill you."],[2675.35,"That's what he's getting at."],[2676.87,"So s so sin comes, sin gets into us."],[2681.43,"Sin is this malign power, is this force, comes and corrupts us, gets into our bodies, gets into our souls, like a poison that weakens us, destroys us, and ultimately kills us."],[2695.77,"Ultimately kills us."],[2698.17,"And so that sting, the sin is taken away."],[2703.49,"The sin is taken away also."],[2705.57,"And the law is taken away at that point also because we don't need it."],[2710.45,"We talked about earlier in 1 Corinthians and in Romans that the Torah is in essence a way of dealing with sin, a way of managing sin and repentance."],[2721.73,"And that's no longer necessary because sin is gone and death is gone and it's all gone."],[2726.77,"And we have life in return through the victory of the Lord, knowing that your labor is not in vain in the Lord."],[2740.85,"And so he now returns to the practical."],[2743.97,"the. Well, if we're not raised from dead, then that means Christ hasn't been raised"],[2752.01,"And if Christ hasn't been raised, then our hope is in vain, then all of this is pointless."],[2755.77,"Everything we're doing is worthless."],[2757.77,"He says, Why am I, as an apostle, risking death to spread the gospel if there's no resurrection?"],[2763.45,"If Christ is not raised."],[2765.21,"Why are you doing any of these things?"],[2767.77,"If Christ is not raised."],[2769.45,"But so now that he's shown that Christ is risen, that we will rise, and he's he's shown the destiny that God has prepared for us."],[2777.71,"Then that gives everything we do in this life, every act of repentance, every work of the Lord, which is how St."],[2784.03,"Paul here describes our good works."],[2785.79,"Notice it's not that, oh, we do something good, we do God a favor. but that we participate in what God is doing in the world"],[2794.07,"Every cold cup of water that we give to someone who is thirsty, everything we do in service to the Lord, all of this now has infinite meaning."],[2803.83,"It's the opposite of vein because of the truth of the resurrection and our destiny in Christ."],[2810.15,"That's what gives everything we do in this life, all the choices we make."],[2814.79,"So much import, ultimate importance."],[2817.91,"And so St."],[2818.95,"Paul ultimately, while through a lot of this chapter, he's been chiding them, he's called them fools, he's criticized them."],[2825.95,"Uh that's ultimately not his intent."],[2828.87,"His intent is to strengthen them, to urge them on to be steadfast, to keep doing what they're doing, do toing keep, to keeping what they're right, to keep follow Christ because of how important it is and running the race because of how great the prize is uh that waits for us at the end."],[2846.67,"So that brings us to the end of chapter 15."],[2849.07,"Uh, so this is a uh good place uh, I think, uh, to end for tonight."],[2854.43,"So I will go ahead uh end here in terms of the recording."],[2860.99,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2868.35,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2873.71,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.44,"lv":2,"h":"15:42-43 - Sown and raised: contrasts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reads v. 42-43: sown in <b>corruption</b>, raised in <b>incorruption</b>; sown in <b>dishonor</b>, raised in <b>glory</b>; sown in <b>weakness</b>, raised in <b>power</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Back to <b>first metaphor</b> - plants, farming, \"sown\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First set = true of our bodies <b>now</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>corrupt</b>: show effects of our sin and sin in the world; not pure</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>dishonor</b>: outside <b>paradise</b>, cut off from God's presence</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>weakness</b>: aches, pains, suffering</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Spirit is willing but the flesh is weak\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Romans</b>: the good I want I don't do, the evil I don't want I do</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Escalation</b>: corruption → <b>incorruption</b>; dishonor → <b>glory</b> (draw close to God again); weakness → <b>power / authority</b></li></ul>"},{"t":141.21,"lv":3,"h":"Glory and power tie back to the angels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Last two (<b>glory</b>, <b>power</b>) = why he <b>introduced angelic beings</b> in the first place</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Glory</b>: angels differ in glory - <b>ranks</b>, how close to God</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>nimbus</b> in icons (West: \"halo\") = saints <b>reflecting God's glory</b> - not their own</li><li class=\"d1\">exhibit A: <b>Moses</b> face to face on Sinai - face shining; Israelites couldn't stand it → <b>veil</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's nimbus differs: name <b>Yahweh</b> in Greek + form of the <b>cross</b> - John: glorified in <b>crucifixion</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's glory = <b>not reflected</b>; saints' = reflected</li><li class=\"d0\">Angels have <b>different bodies</b> - Isaiah, Ezekiel see them look different</li><li class=\"d0\">Our destiny: transformed, participating in His glory → requires <b>new type of body</b></li></ul>"},{"t":316.91,"lv":3,"h":"Power: humans sharing governance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Third element <b>power / authority</b>: our <b>role</b> in creation also like the angels'</li><li class=\"d1\">in presence of God <b>and</b> share His <b>governance</b> of creation</li><li class=\"d0\">= root of Orthodox teaching on the <b>saints</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Human destiny: <b>fallen angels replaced by glorified humanity</b> in governing creation</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Revelation</b> (ch. 20): saints <b>rule and reign</b> with Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ to the <b>twelve</b>: twelve thrones judging <b>twelve tribes</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Promise to <b>Abraham</b>: descendants like the <b>stars</b> = angelic beings</li><li class=\"d0\">Subtle: Israel's <b>72 elders</b> → John sees <b>24 elders</b> = <b>one-third</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Rev 12</b>: one-third of angels fell → <b>replaced by saints in glory</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Our resurrection body <b>suited to this purpose</b> - like angels', animals', plants' bodies suited to theirs</li></ul>"},{"t":519.32,"lv":2,"h":"15:44 - Natural body, spiritual body","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Sown a natural body, raised a spiritual body\" - <b>easily confused</b> portion</li><li class=\"d0\">English <b>\"spiritual\"</b> devalued: \"not religious but spiritual\" = ephemeral, bodiless, feelings</li><li class=\"d1\">then \"spiritual body\" sounds odd; to <b>pagans</b> = <b>oxymoron</b> (body and spirit opposites)</li><li class=\"d0\">Almost every time in Scripture <b>spirit</b> should have a <b>capital S</b></li><li class=\"d1\">rare exceptions: \"touch me... a spirit has not flesh and bones\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Contrast = body characterized by <b>nature</b> vs. body characterized by the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fine-tuning of <b>terrestrial vs. celestial</b></li></ul>"},{"t":692.52,"lv":3,"h":"Permeated by the Spirit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Natural body</b> = part of this world, this age</li><li class=\"d1\">subject to its processes - \"my circulatory system... my <b>dog's</b>... both mammals\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Resurrection body = characterized by being <b>permeated by the Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now Spirit <b>dwells within</b> us - <b>chrismated</b>, anointed, baptized - as a <b>temple</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>permeates</b> us</li><li class=\"d0\">\"What exactly does that mean? I don't know. Neither did St. Paul\" - no \"spiritual science\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same as <b>Rev 21</b> on the creation: <b>no temple</b>, God Himself dwells with us</li><li class=\"d1\">chapter 15: <b>God all in all</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophets: knowledge of God covers the earth <b>as waters cover the sea</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Body: from <b>temple of the Spirit</b> → <b>permeated and transformed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Beyond comprehension - end by giving <b>glory, thanks, praise</b></li></ul>"},{"t":889.23,"lv":2,"h":"15:45 - Adam and the last Adam","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"The first man Adam became a living being, the last Adam became a life-giving spirit\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Infamously difficult</b> - seminary term paper assigned just on this verse</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox Study Bible (NKJV) - he'll <b>retranslate</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"became\"</b> in italics in 2nd clause = <b>not in original</b>, assumed</li><li class=\"d1\">first clause in quotation marks = quote of <b>Gen 2:7</b> (from the <b>Greek</b>)</li></ul>"},{"t":1010.63,"lv":3,"h":"How the New Testament quotes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">No chapter and verse until the <b>16th century</b> - Paul can't say \"see Gen 2:7\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient quoting: <b>first line</b> of a section, or a <b>key sentence</b> that rings a bell</li><li class=\"d1\">then reader goes to the <b>scroll</b> and reads the whole</li><li class=\"d0\">So: look up the <b>source passage</b> - not <b>proof-texting</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they refer to the <b>context</b>, expect you to know or look it up</li></ul>"},{"t":1116.17,"lv":3,"h":"Natural body: the creation of Adam","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Back to <b>original creation</b>: body formed from <b>dust / clay</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God breathes <b>breath of life</b> into his nostrils → becomes a <b>living being</b></li><li class=\"d0\">How we got <b>natural bodies</b>, our life in this world - <b>gift of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Corruption and weakness come later - <b>Genesis 3</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1188.57,"lv":2,"h":"The last Adam = Christ, the life-giving Spirit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Second half: how the <b>resurrection body</b> came to be</li><li class=\"d0\">No verb in Greek: \"the last Adam, <b>the Spirit who gives life</b>\" (capital S)</li><li class=\"d0\">Last Adam = <b>Christ</b>: Adam/Christ link earlier in chapter and <b>Romans 5</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Adam = first man, we inherit <b>nature</b> and natural body by <b>descent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ mortal, corruptible, weak</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ = <b>progenitor</b> of nature we'll have in resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">resurrection body related to <b>Christ's</b> as ours now is to <b>Adam's</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Ginomai</i> (his \"Yenome\") \"became\" can mean <b>come, arrive, enter, appear, start to exist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Gen 2: breath of life <b>enters</b> Adam → natural body</li><li class=\"d1\">spiritual body begins with Christ when <b>the Spirit enters</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1427.09,"lv":3,"h":"Whole Trinity raises Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>All three Persons</b> are agent of Christ's resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: \"no one takes my life... I lay it down, I can <b>take it up</b>\" (John)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Father</b> raised Christ (Paul); <b>Holy Spirit</b> raised Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Here: Spirit as agent → involved in <b>our</b> resurrection</li><li class=\"d0\">Baptized into Christ, <b>put on Christ</b>, received the Spirit</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Already / not yet</b>: <b>Ephesians</b> - already seated with Christ, ruling and reigning</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>already resurrected</b> - received Spirit, died and rose in baptism</li><li class=\"d1\">awaiting reality in soul and body at <b>glorious appearing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">His translation: \"the first man Adam became a living being, <b>and into the last Adam came a life-giving Spirit</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1582.44,"lv":2,"h":"15:46-49 - Natural first, then spiritual","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Not first the spiritual, but the natural, afterward the spiritual\" - an <b>order</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Against <b>pre-existence of souls</b>: Mormons, <b>Origenists</b>, <b>Gnostics</b>, Platonists</li><li class=\"d1\">divine existence → fell → escape</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: natural first; <b>came into existence at birth, period</b>; spiritual <b>comes next</b> if in Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">\"First man of the earth, made of dust; second man the <b>Lord from heaven</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Adam made <b>out of the earth</b>, then <b>placed in paradise</b> - not created there</li><li class=\"d1\">after transgression <b>removed back</b> into this world</li></ul>"},{"t":1693.29,"lv":3,"h":"\"The Lord from heaven\" = Yahweh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Against claims Paul didn't think Christ is <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gen 2 Hebrew uses <b>Yahweh</b>; Greek translation uses <b>Kyrios</b> (Lord)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"From heaven\"</b> → not just master or ruler: Christ is <b>Yahweh come from his heavenly place</b> to become man</li><li class=\"d0\">Written <b>mid-50s</b> - Christ's divinity did <b>not develop over centuries</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chapter 8</b>: Paul inserted Christ into the <b>Shema</b> - \"Yahweh your God, Yahweh is one\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1815.97,"lv":3,"h":"15:48-49 - Image of the heavenly man","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"As is the man of dust, so are those made of dust; as is the heavenly man, so are the heavenly... bear the image of the heavenly man\"</li><li class=\"d0\">End of Gen 3: Adam brought forth a son <b>in his own image</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Borne Adam's image: body, soul, life in the world → we'll be <b>like Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Specifically our <b>bodies</b> like <b>Christ's body</b> in resurrection - not just virtue or knowledge</li></ul>"},{"t":1882.35,"lv":2,"h":"15:50 - Flesh and blood cannot inherit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God, nor does corruption inherit incorruption\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's <b>distinction</b>: <b>body</b> vs. <b>flesh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">been talking about all kinds of <b>bodies</b> - now \"flesh\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not contradicting: <b>not</b> \"no body enters\" / soul escapes</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Flesh</b> = sinful, corrupt element of our body</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Blood</b> = <b>life</b> in the OT - \"life is in the blood\"</li><li class=\"d1\">flesh and blood = our life in this world, body <b>as corrupt</b></li><li class=\"d1\">parallel: <b>corruption</b> doesn't inherit incorruption</li><li class=\"d0\">Not bodies but the <b>corruption</b> of bodies</li></ul>"},{"t":1980.69,"lv":3,"h":"Garments of skin and mortality","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">At expulsion Adam and Eve receive a <b>different type of body</b></li><li class=\"d1\">created <b>immortal</b>, became <b>mortal</b> - not always mortal, with God keeping them off cliffs / <b>elephants</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers and <b>Second Temple</b> sources: <b>garments of skin</b> (Gen 3)</li><li class=\"d1\">not just leather clothes after fig leaves</li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>killed animals</b> - show them <b>death</b>; garments of <b>flesh</b>, bloody</li><li class=\"d0\">→ flesh and blood in Paul = mortal bodies: corruption, death, pain, suffering</li></ul>"},{"t":2082.34,"lv":2,"h":"Mortality as a gift to enable repentance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God gave mortality to enable <b>repentance</b>, change, transformation</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Demons</b>: no repentance - <b>immortally fixed</b> in rebellion</li><li class=\"d0\">Extra <b>grace</b> to humanity: not left immortal - else \"like the demons\", <b>forever in rebellion</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Mortality brings <b>mutability</b> - suffer, change, repent - <b>a gift</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. John of Damascus</b>, funeral hymns: turned back to the ground</li><li class=\"d0\">Destiny we're created for <b>kept open</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not all repent: some <b>hardened</b> → share demons' fate at the end - hatred of God</li><li class=\"d0\">For those who repent: <b>forgiveness</b> appointed</li><li class=\"d0\">Flesh and blood, mortality, corruption <b>taken away</b>; <b>death serves its purpose</b></li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't disappear but has <b>no sting</b>, no victory, no power</li><li class=\"d1\">purifies us, delivers from sin and corruption into <b>salvation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2264.92,"lv":2,"h":"15:51-53 - Mystery: we shall be changed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be <b>changed</b>... in a moment, twinkling of an eye, at the <b>last trumpet</b>... this corruptible must put on incorruption\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not all die: <b>last generation</b> alive at <b>glorious appearing</b> - same change</li><li class=\"d0\">Returns to <b>general resurrection and last judgment</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2318.64,"lv":3,"h":"The last trumpet and Rosh Hashanah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Trumpet also in <b>2 Thessalonians</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Feast of Trumpets</b> = <b>Rosh Hashanah</b> (\"head of the year\"), <b>1st of Tishri</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First Tishri: <b>Noah</b> born, flood ended, world began again</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>David</b> crowned king of Judah; all Judah's kings (incl. <b>Messiah</b>) crowned/began reign</li><li class=\"d1\">→ day of the Lord: age to come begins with <b>trumpet blast</b>; <b>Christ's full reign</b> begins</li><li class=\"d0\">Early <b>September</b> (lunar calendar) - why Orthodox church new year <b>Sept 1</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Theory: Christ born <b>1 Tishri, 3 BC</b> (<b>Revelation 12</b>) - not convinced, will cover at Rev 12</li><li class=\"d0\">Trumpet = symbol of <b>end of this age</b>, start of age to come</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Garments of flesh</b> reversed: mortality <b>clothed with immortality</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Tishri 1 = thought <b>sixth day of creation</b> (humanity made) → <b>new sixth day</b>, recreated in resurrection</li></ul>"},{"t":2535.66,"lv":2,"h":"15:54-57 - Death swallowed up in victory","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Death is swallowed up in victory. O death, where is your sting? O Hades, where is your victory? The sting of death is sin, and the strength of sin is the law. Thanks be to God who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Already / not yet</b> completed at the resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">sin gone, death gone, <b>evil powers</b> put down; Christ <b>all in all</b>, hands kingdom to Father</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>three enemies</b> from last time - <b>finally, permanently defeated and destroyed</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2612.49,"lv":3,"h":"Sting of death is sin; strength of sin is the law","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Strength of sin is the law</b>: law <b>shows us sin</b>, points to its power</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Romans</b>: reading the commandments led to temptation - makes <b>him</b> evil, <b>commandment good</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sting</b> = wound with <b>poison</b> - snake, scorpion, spider</li><li class=\"d1\">sin = <b>malign power</b>, corrupts body and soul like poison → <b>kills</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sting removed</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Law</b> taken away too - <b>Torah</b> = managing sin and repentance; no longer needed</li></ul>"},{"t":2726.77,"lv":2,"h":"15:58 - Your labor is not in vain","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Returns to the <b>practical</b>: if no resurrection, Christ not raised, hope <b>in vain</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why risk death as an apostle? why do any of it?</li><li class=\"d0\">Now Christ risen, we will rise → <b>every act of repentance</b>, \"<b>work of the Lord</b>\" has meaning</li><li class=\"d1\">not doing God a favor - <b>participating</b> in what God is doing</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>cold cup of water</b> - infinite meaning, <b>ultimate importance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Despite chiding and calling them <b>fools</b> - intent is to <b>strengthen</b>, urge <b>steadfast</b></li><li class=\"d1\">keep following Christ, <b>run the race</b> - the <b>prize</b> at the end</li><li class=\"d0\">End of <b>chapter 15</b></li></ul>"}]});
